theory.intro:
To sketch $y = f(x) + g(x)$ or $y = f(x) - g(x)$, add or subtract the $y$-values, called **ordinates**, of the two graphs at each $x$. Work from key $x$-values across the shared domain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-3.6, xmax=3.6, ymin=-2.2, ymax=3.6, samples=200, clip=false, xtick=\empty, ytick=\empty, axis line style={-{Stealth}}]
\addplot[dashed, blue, <->, domain=-3:3] {0.6*x + 0.5};
\addplot[dashed, red, <->, domain=-3:3] {0.9*sin(deg(1.5*x))};
\addplot[thick, black, <->, domain=-3:3] {0.6*x + 0.5 + 0.9*sin(deg(1.5*x))};
\draw[gray, dotted] (axis cs:1.3,0) -- (axis cs:1.3,2.116);
\draw[gray, dotted] (axis cs:-1.3,0) -- (axis cs:-1.3,-1.116);
\fill[blue] (axis cs:1.3,1.28) circle (2pt);
\fill[red] (axis cs:1.3,0.836) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (axis cs:1.3,2.116) circle (2pt);
\fill[blue] (axis cs:-1.3,-0.28) circle (2pt);
\fill[red] (axis cs:-1.3,-0.836) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (axis cs:-1.3,-1.116) circle (2pt);
\node[below] at (axis cs:1.3,0) {$a$};
\node[above left] at (axis cs:-1.3,0) {$b$};
\node[right, blue] at (axis cs:-3.5,1.5) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[right, red] at (axis cs:-3.5,2.3) {$y=g(x)$};
\node[right] at (axis cs:-3.5,3.1) {$y=f(x)+g(x)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
1. **Identify key landmarks on the component graphs: zeroes, intersection points, turning points, and vertical asymptotes.**
$x$-intercepts occur where $f(x) = g(x) \implies x = x^3 \implies x(x^2 - 1) = 0$.
$x = -1, 0, 1$.
2. **Add or subtract the ordinates ($y$-values) at these key $x$-values to locate benchmark points.**
For $0 < x < 1$, $x > x^3$, so $y > 0$.
For $x > 1$, $x^3 > x$, so $y < 0$.
Since $f(-x) - g(-x) = -x - (-x^3) = -(x - x^3)$, the function is odd (point symmetry about origin).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-2, xmax=2, ymin=-2.5, ymax=2.5, samples=150, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}]
\addplot[dashed, blue, <->, domain=-1.8:1.8] {x} node[pos=0.9, above left] {$y=x$};
\addplot[dashed, red, <->, domain=-1.35:1.35] {x*x*x} node[pos=0.9, right] {$y=x^3$};
\addplot[thick, black, <->, domain=-1.5:1.5] {x - x*x*x} node[pos=0.85, below right] {$y=x-x^3$};
\fill (axis cs:-1,0) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$-1$};
\fill (axis cs:1,0) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$1$};
\fill (axis cs:0,0) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$O$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$x$-intercepts at $(-1, 0), (0, 0), (1, 0)$; odd symmetry about the origin.

1. **Identify key landmarks on the component graphs: zeroes, intersection points, turning points, and vertical asymptotes.**
The critical boundary points are $x = -1$ and $x = 3$.
2. **Add or subtract the ordinates ($y$-values) at these key $x$-values to locate benchmark points.**
For $x < -1$: $y = -(x + 1) - (x - 3) = -2x + 2$
For $-1 \le x \le 3$: $y = (x + 1) - (x - 3) = 4$
For $x > 3$: $y = (x + 1) + (x - 3) = 2x - 2$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-4, xmax=6, ymin=-1, ymax=10, samples=50, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}]
\addplot[thick, black, <->] coordinates {(-3,8) (-1,4) (3,4) (5,8)};
\fill (axis cs:-1,4) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$(-1,4)$};
\fill (axis cs:3,4) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(3,4)$};
\fill (axis cs:0,4) circle (2pt) node[below] {$(0,4)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Minimum value is $4$, occurring on the interval $[-1, 3]$.
